---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Overhead Press One Rep Max Calculator — Estimate Your OHP 1RM"
  description="Estimate your strict overhead press one rep max from a set of 1–12 reps. Six formulas, what makes a strict rep, and how to plan small loadable jumps."
  path="/overhead-press-calculator/"
  name="Overhead Press Calculator"
  h1="Overhead Press One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your strict standing overhead press max from a recent set. The press moves in small steps, so the rounding of your training weights matters."
  exercise="overhead-press"
  lift="Overhead press"
  example={{ weight: 115, reps: 5, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.bench, LINKS.row, LINKS.percent, LINKS.plates]}
  sources={['lesuer', 'reynolds', 'hprc']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="ohp-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="ohp-estimate-heading">How to estimate your overhead press max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Press a weight you can lift for 3–6 strict reps, standing, with no leg drive, and enter the
      weight and reps above. For example, 115 lb × 5 estimates an overhead press max of roughly
      129–137 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of the six formulas was built specifically for the overhead press, and the validation
      studies we cite tested the bench press, squat, deadlift and leg press. The estimate is still a
      reasonable guide, but expect more individual variation than on the bench press.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ohp-strict-heading">
    <h2 id="ohp-strict-heading">Strict press vs. push press</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Strict press:</strong> knees stay locked; the bar starts from the shoulders and is pressed overhead to lockout. This is what the calculator assumes.</li>
      <li><strong>Push press:</strong> a dip and drive from the legs launches the bar. If any rep in your set used leg drive, the estimate will read high for a strict max.</li>
      <li><strong>Excessive layback</strong> turns the press into a steep incline press and also inflates the number. Keep the torso as upright as your technique allows.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ohp-jumps-heading">
    <h2 id="ohp-jumps-heading">Small jumps matter on the press</h2>
    <p>
      A 5 lb jump on a 135 lb press is almost 4% — far bigger, relatively, than the same jump on a
      deadlift. 1RMHub&rsquo;s training tables round <em>down</em> to the smallest standard step
      (5 lb or 2.5 kg). If your gym has fractional plates, set them in the
      <a href="/plate-calculator/">plate calculator</a> to load exactly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ohp-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="ohp-safety-heading">Overhead press safety</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Press inside a rack with the safety pins set just below the shoulders, or with a spotter for a true max attempt.</li>
      <li>Brace the trunk and squeeze the glutes to limit layback on heavy reps.</li>
      <li>Stop the set if your lower back starts to arch to finish reps.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ohp-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="ohp-faq-heading">Overhead press max: common questions</h2>
    <h3>How do I calculate my overhead press max by hand?</h3>
    <p>
      Use the Epley formula: <strong>1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</strong>. For 115 lb × 5:
      115 × (1 + 5 ÷ 30) = <strong>134.2 lb</strong>. The calculator runs five more published
      formulas and shows the range between them.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it work for seated or dumbbell presses?</h3>
    <p>
      Only for the same lift. Seated, standing, barbell and dumbbell presses are different lifts, so
      a set of one predicts a max for that one. Do not use a seated set to predict a standing max.
    </p>
    <h3>How often should I update my press max?</h3>
    <p>
      Whenever you complete a new hard set of 3–6 strict reps. Because the press moves in small
      steps, a new estimate every few weeks or at the end of a training block is usually enough.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
